  "plan": {
    "goal": "Perfuse a liver-on-chip to reproduce the reported hepatic sinusoidal wall shear stress of 0.05 Pa.",
    "rationale": "Hepatic sinusoidal wall shear stress is very low in vivo (~0.05–0.15 Pa; the HepG2 registry lists 0.05–0.15 Pa as the physiological shear range). I set the target to the lower bound, 0.05 Pa, to reproduce the low-shear sinusoidal environment. Using a standard liver-on-chip channel (400 µm wide × 100 µm high, 20 mm long, PDMS) with culture-medium viscosity (1e-3 Pa·s), the flow rate required to achieve 0.05 Pa is 2 µL/min (computed via flow_rate_for_shear_stress). This yields a Reynolds number of 0.13 (laminar), a pressure drop of 20 Pa (trivial for a syringe pump), and a 24 s residence time. Seeding HepG2 at 5e4 cells/cm² over the 0.08 cm² channel gives 4000 cells; oxygen supply exceeds demand ~42×, so the culture is not hypoxic.",
    "chip": {
      "width_um": 400.0,
      "height_um": 100.0,
